# PHP brace placement

When writing or editing PHP, put the opening `{` on its **own line** after the declaration or control header. Closing `}` stays aligned with the keyword that opened the block.

Applies to: `class`, `function` / methods, `if` / `else` / `elseif`, `foreach`, `for`, `while`, `do`, `switch`, `try` / `catch` / `finally`, and anonymous functions when they use a braced body.

```php
// BAD — K&R / same-line brace
if ($x) {
    ...
}

class Foo {
    public function bar() {
        ...
    }
}

// GOOD — brace on next line
if ($x)
{
    ...
}

class Foo
{
    public function bar()
    {
        ...
    }
}
```

`else` / `elseif` / `catch` / `finally` follow the same pattern: keyword on one line, `{` on the next.

```php
// GOOD
try
{
    ...
}
catch (Throwable $e)
{
    ...
}
```

One-line closures with `fn` need no braces. For array literals and other non-block syntax, this rule does not apply.
